content.paragraph:
Let $W = \text{span}\left\{ \textbf{w} \right\}$. Choose a basis $\left\{ \textbf{v}_{1}\text{,}\textbf{v}_{2}\text{,}\ldots\text{,}\textbf{v}_{n - 1} \right\}$ for $W^{\bot}$. Create the matrix $B = \begin{bmatrix}
        {\textbf{v}_{1}}^{T} \\
        {\textbf{v}_{2}}^{T} \\
         \vdots \\
        {\textbf{v}_{n - 1}}^{T}
        \end{bmatrix}$. Notice $B\textbf{u} = \textbf{0}$ if and only if $\textbf{u}$ is orthogonal to a set of basis vectors for $W^{\bot}$, which happens if and only if $\textbf{u}$ is in $W$. Set $M = B^{T}B$. Then $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = \textbf{u}^{T}B^{T}B\textbf{u} = {(B\textbf{u})}^{T}B\textbf{u}$. By Theorem 1, ${(B\textbf{u})}^{T}B\textbf{u} = 0$ if and only if $B\textbf{u} = 0$, and hence $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = 0$ if and only if $\textbf{u} \in W$. But there are only two vectors in $W$ consisting of zeros and ones: $1\textbf{w} = \textbf{w}$ and $0\textbf{w} = \textbf{0}$. Thus we can conclude that if $\textbf{u}^{T}M\textbf{u} = 0$, but $\textbf{u}^{T}\textbf{u} \neq 0$, then $\textbf{u} = \textbf{w}$. See Figure 6.

